  "query_preview": "..\n\nA tropical airmass is in place across the Midwest, as the 00z KDVN \nRAOB reported an extreme 700 MB dew pt of 11 C. Southeasterly \ntrajectories beneath an upper-level ridge axis over the northern \nMississippi River Valley will keep the deep moisture south of the \nMichigan border today. A strong upper-level wave coming out of \neastern Colorado early this morning will pool another round of deep \nmoisture (PW values 2.25 to 2.5 inches), which will make a run \ntoward- and at least impact locations near-the southern Michigan \nborder by Saturday evening. Several hi-res model solutions hold the \ninstability gradient just south of the Michigan border. However, The \nnotable exceptions are the NAM and Canadian models, which are \nstronger and farther north with the shortwave/MCV. This would \npresent a heavy rain threat with MUcapes over 1000 J/kg. 700 MB dew \npts are seen rising to around 10 C, which is near record high from a \nclimatological prospective. However, with the warm and most airmass \nin the mid-levels, mid-level lapse rates are weak. Local \nprobabilistic guidance suggests NAM is an outlier with the amount of \ninstability and excessive rainfall. Most solutions indicating \nrainfall amounts generally under 1 inch, except perhaps right near \nthe southern Michigan border. 18z Euro ensemble members suggest a 10-\n20 percent chance for 24-hour qpf to reach or exceed 1 inch across \nall of southeast Michigan. However, the 00z UKMET is also bullish on \nheavy rainfall, indicating a swath of 2+ inches of rain across the \nsouth half of the CWA/Detroit Metro area. The SPC day 2 outlook has \nhighlighted a marginal risk of severe for Lenawee and parts of \nWashtenaw/Monroe counties. If a stronger circulation and \ncorresponding enhanced 0-6 KM bulk shear verify, then damaging winds \nand even a tornado would be possible. Obviously, a good deal of \nuncertainty exists this far out. \n\nAssuming low clouds and/or any fog this morning dissipate by mid-\nmorning and a canopy of higher clouds from storms over the Ohio \nValley is not too impactful, highs in the mid 80s seem reasonable \ntoday. Similar temperatures are expected for Saturday as clouds \nthicken up during the day and potential showers arrive by day's end \nwith light southeasterly flow at the surface. \n\nModest height falls for the second half of the weekend into early \nnext week will bring modest troughing over the Great Lakes region \nextending east into New England. It looks like a cold front tracking \nthrough Sunday into Sunday evening will be support numerous showers \nand strong thunderstorms before dry weather returns for Monday and \nTuesday as large high pressure over the Western Ohio Valley extends \ninto southeast Michigan.",
